From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000019 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Port Bot <port@local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 22:07:32 +0200
Subject: 0019: pluggable transports for the pod Terminal tab, with
 multi-target via entries

The pod "Terminal" tab (frontend/public/components/pod-connect.tsx) hardcodes
exec-over-websocket as its only way to reach a container, so a dynamic plugin
cannot offer an alternative (e.g. a VNC framebuffer) for pods that expose one.

Add a small, transport-agnostic extension point: console resolves extensions of
type stei.gr/pod-connect-transport, asks each one whether it can serve the
currently selected container, and - if any can - renders a second "via" dropdown
next to the container dropdown, listing those transports plus the built-in
"Terminal". While a non-terminal transport is selected the exec websocket is not
opened at all and the extension's component replaces the terminal body.

A transport that can reach more than one distinct thing on the same container
(e.g. several VNC endpoints) can declare a targets(obj, containerName) function
returning one entry per option; the "via" dropdown then expands to one entry
per target instead of one per transport, labelled "<transport label> (<target
label>)", and the extension's component receives the chosen one as targetId.
A transport that declares no targets, or at most one, behaves exactly as
before - just its own label in the dropdown.

Console core stays free of any VNC/noVNC knowledge; that lives in the
terminal console plugin, which contributes the only such extension today.
When no plugin contributes one the toolbar and behaviour are unchanged.

The exec session waits for extension resolution before connecting, so a
container a plugin claims never opens a pod exec that is torn down again a
moment later, and the "exit\r" teardown tolerates a socket that is still
CONNECTING, which a transport switch mid-handshake can produce.

A transport can also register toolbar actions (e.g. "send this key
combination to the remote session") via a new onActionsChange callback on
PodConnectTransportProps. When non-empty, they render as a menu in the
toolbar, immediately left of the existing Expand/Collapse control; console
resets them whenever the active transport or target changes.
